    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5512736/construction-manager0081585": {
      "title": "Construction Manager (0081585)",
      "description": "Construction Manager (0081585) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02179 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division Administrative Affairs - Facilities Planning & Construction Opening Date 10/09/2026 Closing Date 10/26/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0081585 Description Title: Construction Manager Position Number: 0081585 Hiring Unit: Administrative Affairs, Office of Facilities Planning and Management (OFPM) Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: October 9, 2026 Closing Date: October 26, 2026 Band: B Salary: salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-Time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Funding: General Funds Other Conditions: Appointment to begin approximately November 16, 2026, pending position clearance and availability of funds. Position Overview: The University of Hawai'i at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public liberal arts and sciences university on the Island of Hawai'i, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawai'i System, UH Hilo offers high-quality undergraduate and select postgraduate education, with distinctive strengths in areas such as marine science, geology, astronomy, and Hawaiian language revitalization, while advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity that support community needs, environmental stewardship, Indigenous knowledge systems, and workforce development for the State of Hawai'i. The Division of Administrative Affairs provides the administrative and operational foundation that enables UH Hilo’s academic mission to thrive. The division oversees critical non-academic functions including budget development, fiscal management, human resources, facilities planning and construction, physical plant management, information technology, campus security, and environmental safety and health, ensuring that campus operations remain efficient, compliant, and aligned with institutional priorities. The Office of Facilities Planning and Management (OFPM) plays a central role in translating UH Hilo’s strategic goals into the physical spaces and infrastructure that support student learning, faculty research, and community engagement. By integrating long-range capital planning with maintenance management, the office enhances campus stewardship and ensures that UH Hilo’s physical environment evolves in alignment with its academic mission and institutional priorities. Its work provides the essential foundation upon which teaching, research, student life, and community engagement can thrive. The Construction Manager contributes directly to this mission by providing technical expertise, project leadership, and operational oversight of campus construction and renovation projects. Working within the Facilities Planning and Management Office, this position ensures that all projects are executed safely, efficiently, and in compliance with UH System standards and state requirements. UH Hilo’s Construction Manager collaborates with contractors, consultants, and campus stakeholders to maintain project schedules, budgets, and quality standards that directly contributes to UH Hilo’s long-term vision by helping shape a campus that supports academic excellence, student success, and long-term institutional goals. Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions): Design Review & Technical Guidance 1. *Reviews architectural and engineering plans and specifications for constructability, completeness, and adherence to UH Hilo standards prior to bidding; evaluates proposed materials and equipment for durability and long-term maintenance cost. 2. *Collaborates with the Director, Project Managers, and consultants to clarify scope of work, resolve technical questions, and interpret design intent; advises on phasing, site logistics, sequencing, and long-lead procurement. Construction Oversight & Field Coordination 1. *Provides daily oversight and coordination of construction activity across contractors and subcontractors on multiple concurrent projects, safeguarding scope, cost, quality, and schedule outcomes. 2. *Plans and coordinates construction impacts to minimize disruption to academic, research, residential, and event operations; develops and communicates utility outage, shutdown, access, and noise plans with affected stakeholders. 3. *Conducts daily site visits to inspect work in progress, documents field observations, and verifies conformance to contract plans, specifications, and quality standards; reports on delays and impacts. 4. *Monitors the contractor's construction schedule against contract milestones; identifies potential problems and impact to schedule; recommends corrective actions and requires recovery plans to maintain project momentum and compliance. 5. *Maintains complete and current project documentation, including daily reports, correspondence, submittals, and records that build the compliance and closeout record. Quality Management 1. *Coordinates and manages inspections, materials testing, and special inspections; assesses and recommends corrective action for deficiencies and non-conforming work. 2. *Reviews submittals, shop drawings, samples, and RFIs for constructability and maintainability. Cost Management 1. *Reviews proposed change orders to determine impact on budget and schedule; prepares cost analyses to support approval or negotiation aligned with campus priorities. 2. *Reviews and verifies contractor payment applications against the schedule of values and work in place. 3. *Identifies and flags potential cost overruns, differing site conditions, and out-of-scope work early. Safety & Regulatory Compliance 1. *Verifies contractor site-safety and health programs and monitors job site compliance with OSHA and Environmental Health and Safety (EHS) requirements. 2. *Monitors contractor compliance with applicable building codes, accessibility standards, and environmental regulations. 3. *Enforces job site safety requirements, including site barricades, traffic control, and emergency access lanes. 4. *Responds to job site emergencies and safety incidents to protect life safety and minimize project impact; documents and reports findings to the Director. Commissioning, Closeout & Turnover 1. *Coordinates building commissioning and systems testing for operational readiness. 2. *Manages final inspections and punch-list closure through final completion. 3. *Administers the warranty period and ensures all warranty work is completed before warranty expiration. 4. *Coordinates turnover, training, documentation, and system integration with Facilities Maintenance. 5. Captures lessons learned and performance data to inform design standards, lifecycle, and long-term capital planning. 6. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Education and Professional Work Experience: 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in construction management or project management or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for construction management and/or project management; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of construction management as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with construction management. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, and apply spreadsheet software. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Supplemental Minimum Qualifications: 1. Considerable working knowledge of County of Hawai’i building code requirements. 2. Considerable experience preparing and reviewing construction cost estimates, including detailed quantity takeoffs and cost analysis. 3. Extensive experience negotiating contractor change order proposals, and preparing written cost analysis to support cost reasonableness and contract time extensions. 4. Extensive working knowledge and ability to conduct construction site inspections, monitor construction progress, identify deficiencies, and ensure conformity with plans, specifications, schedules, and quality standards. 5. Extensive working knowledge of construction methods, materials, building codes, and industry standards. 6. Ability to interpret and review architectural and engineering drawings, technical specifications, schedules, cost estimates, and related construction documents. 7. Proven ability to facilitate and maintain effective working relationships with contractors, subcontractors, design consultants, inspectors, and stakeholders on construction projects. 8. Proficiency in project management software program(s) to track progress, milestones, and project performance for multiple construction projects. 9. Considerable experience managing contractor force-account work. 10. Working knowledge of OSHA regulations, accessibility standards, and environmental health and safety regulations applicable to construction in Hawai’i. 11. Experience reviewing contractor payment applications, change orders, and contract modifications for accuracy and compliance. 12. Ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously, set priorities, meet deadlines, and work independently with sound judgment. 13. Demonstrate strong communication and conflict resolution skills. 14. Strong problem-solving and decision-making abilities. 15. Physical demands: standing, walking, and climbing. 16. Comfortable working outdoors in varying conditions with appropriate PPE. Desirable Qualifications: 1. Knowledge of University of Hawai’i or the State of Hawai’i construction procurement and contract administration procedures and practices. 2. Knowledge of University of Hawai’i or an institution of higher learning, facilities planning and construction procedures. 3. Extensive working knowledge of DCCA contractor licensing requirements applicable to construction projects. 4. Extensive working knowledge of the Davis-Bacon Act, and Chapter 104 prevailing wage requirements. 5. Two (2) years of construction management and/or project management experience on government construction projects exceeding $2 million. 6. Working knowledge of Trimble Unity Construct. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: 1. Cover letter explaining how each minimum and desirable qualification is met; 2. Current resume; 3. The names and addresses, email addresses and telephone numbers of three [3] current professional references; 4. Transcript(s) showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment. Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Ken Kaleiwahea, kjakalei@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination on the basis of race, sex, gender identity and expression, age, religion, color, national origin, ancestry, citizenship, disability, genetic information, marital status, breastfeeding, income assignment for child support, arrest and court record (except as permissible under State law), sexual orientation, domestic or sexual violence victim status, national guard absence, or status as a covered veteran. For more information or inquiries regarding these policies, please refer to the following link: http://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/eeo-coordinators/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5513444/graduate-assistant-hauoli-mau-loa": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant (Hau'oli Mau Loa)",
      "description": "Graduate Assistant (Hau'oli Mau Loa) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02182 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division College of Natural and Health Sciences - Biology Opening Date 10/12/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: GA11 Description Position Number: GA11 Hiring Unit: College of Health & Natural Sciences, TCBES Location: UH-Hilo Date Posted: October 12, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment until filled, first review date on or about December 15, 2026 Salary Information: GA11-15 $2,429/mo $ 29,148/annual Monthly Type: 11 Month Full Time/Part Time: Full Time - Part Time Equivalency - 50% Temporary/Permanent: Temporary - Not to Exceed: July 31, 2028 The overall goal of the Hau‘oli Mau Loa graduate fellowship program in TCBES is to help develop the next generation of environmental leaders (e.g., conservation biologists or environmental scientists) in Hawai‘i. Other Conditions: Appointment to begin approximately August 2027, pending availability of funding. Three graduate assistantships available for students dedicated to careers in conservation biology and environmental science in the state of Hawai‘i. Each assistantship comes with generous support for two years (2nd year pending satisfactory academic performance), including a Graduate Assistantship (salary plus fringe benefits), a full tuition remission, and additional funds to be used for attendance costs (e.g., books, fees, professional development, including travel to local and national conferences, and supplies). CRITERIA: (1) good academic standing (minimum GPA of 3.0 throughout the fellowship); (2) from Hawai‘i, as evidenced by having graduated from a high school in Hawai‘i; (3) pursuing a M.S. degree in TCBES; (4) track record of supporting Hawai‘i’s environment (e.g., internships, volunteer opportunities) and serving in a leadership capacity or demonstrated leadership potential (academic, athletic, extracurricular, and/or professional); and (5) commitment to attaining and pursuing a long term career in conservation biology and environmental science in Hawai‘i. Duties and Responsibilities: 1. Work with thesis advisor or internship mentor, where duties may include, but not limited to collecting data, processing samples, analyzing and interpreting data, writing project reports and/or thesis/manuscript, presenting results at meetings (i.e., community, local, state, or international conferences), mentoring undergraduate student assistants, developing final products for the project/internship, and other assigned tasks relevant to the position. Minimum Qualifications 1. Accepted to or currently enrolled in the UH Hilo TCBES MS Program To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: 1. Cover letter explaining how you satisfy the minimum qualifications. You must clearly express your commitment to and leadership in supporting Hawaii’s environment; 2. Curriculum Vitae or resume; 3. Names, telephone numbers and email addresses of three [3] professional references; 4. Transcript(s) (official transcripts will be required prior to hiring; unofficial transcripts are acceptable for your initial application. Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Pat Hart, Associate Director TCBES Graduate Program; pjhart@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5513915/graduate-teaching-assistant-tcbes": {
      "title": "Graduate Teaching Assistant (TCBES)",
      "description": "Graduate Teaching Assistant (TCBES) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02184 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division College of Natural and Health Sciences - Biology Opening Date 10/12/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: GA09 Description Title: Graduate Teaching Assistant Position Number: GA09 Hiring Unit: College of Natural & Health Sciences (TCBES) Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: October 12, 2026 Closing Date: continuous recruitment until filled, first review December 15, 2026 Salary Information: GA09-12, $1,845/mo. $22,140 (annual) Monthly Type: 9 Month Full Time/Part Time: Part Time Equivalency - 50% FTE Temporary/Permanent: Temporary - Not to Exceed: 7/31/28 Other Conditions: Appointment to begin approximately August 2027, pending position clearance and availability of funding. Graduate Assistants will be given initial one year terms, appointments may be extended based on satisfactory performance and availability of funds. Priority will be given to students in their 1st and 2nd year of the TCBES program. Graduate Assistants will be under the direct supervision of the Graduate Assistant Coordinator of the TCBES program, as well as the supervisor of the specific academic unit under which the candidate will be functioning. Duties and Responsibilities 1. Teach or assist programs in the department to which the candidate will be assigned. 2. Work closely with the appropriate departmental Graduate Assistant Coordinator throughout the semester. Duties could include, but not limited to data gathering, course planning, preparation, grading, classroom management, student advising. 3. Work with students to enhance their comprehension of assigned reading materials or advise on academic matters. 4. Conduct informal discussion groups to ensure understanding of lectures and lab assignments. 5. Supervise students in the laboratory, as well as on field excursions. 6. Train and monitor laboratory users for compliance with basic safety and scientific procedures. 7. Ensure proper maintenance of laboratory and field equipment and supplies. 8. Transport students in a 12- or 15-passenger van, if needed. 9. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications 1. Accepted to or currently enrolled in the UH Hilo TCBES MS Program. 2. Bachelor’s degree in Biology, Marine Science, Geography, Agriculture, or a related Environmental Science or Environmental Studies field or equivalent education/training or experience. 3. Significant knowledge of, and ability to teach topics in biology, environmental science, marine science, agriculture, and/or geospatial techniques (GIS) at the undergraduate level. 4. Demonstrated ability to communicate well. Desirable Qualifications 1. Previous experience teaching, leading discussions, record keeping, or supervising undergraduate students in a classroom, laboratory, or field setting. 2. Previous experience in grading and evaluating student work. 3. Previous experience with curriculum development, tutoring and/or outreach either in formal or informal education. 4. Previous experience with community building, conflict resolution, and people management. 5. Previous experience with quantitative methods such as statistics, advanced GIS, remote sensing, and/or computer programming. 6. Previous experience conducting independent scientific research. 7. Previous experience with website management. 8. Valid driver’s license. Specifics to Departments 1. Biology: GA position provides support for BIOL 280, BIOL 281L, and BIOL 481L. Duties for BIOL 280 include, but are not limited to: 1) Grading homework assignments and exams. 2) Giving a guest lecture. 3) Holding office hours. 4) Preparing exam review study sessions. 5) Assisting students with obtaining and using software (Minitab, R), including troubleshooting or providing directions. 6) Providing guidance to students on their group research project. Duties for BIOL 281L include, but are not limited to: 1) Attending each lab and assisting with driving students to the field site in an 12-passenger van. 2) Assisting students on lab assignments and data collection in the field and data analysis. 3) Holding office hours. 4) Helping grade lab handouts. Duties for BIOL 481L include, but are not limited to: 1) Helping out with the field work by preparing field materials for surveys/experiments. 2) Driving the 12-passenger van with students to and from field sites. 3) Assisting with project development, data collection in the field and data analysis. 4) Providing feedback on their draft manuscripts. 5) Holding office hours. 6) Giving one lecture and one lesson in the lecture portion of the course. 2. Geography & Environmental Science: GA position provides academic support and technical assistance for the Spatial Data Analysis & Visualization (SDAV) geospatial teaching facility. Duties may include, but not limited to: 1) Providing undergraduate and graduate tutorial support for students enrolled in various geospatial classes (including GEOG201, GEOG280, GEOG328, GEOG470, GEOG480, GEOG 481, CBES 670, CBES681), and for students needing to use GIS and geospatial techniques in class or independent research/thesis projects. 2) Holding regular office hours and being familiar with geospatial software and relevant course assignments to effectively answer student questions. 3) Providing technical troubleshooting support for computer and software licensing issues in the SDAV teaching facility. 4) Providing and facilitating student and faculty check out of equipment (GPS units, field tapes, cameras, weather data collectors, etc.) from the SDAV laboratory. 5) Assisting with SDAV-related community and K12 outreach events, including demonstrations of geospatial datasets and technologies for local schools, etc. This position may also provide support to the TCBES Graduate Program. Possible tasks include maintaining and updating the TCBES website and social media accounts, assisting with TCBES vehicle maintenance, rentals, and payments, assisting with managing the TCBES workroom, and assisting with the TCBES Newsletter. Duties will vary on a weekly basis, but the GA will spend the majority of their time supporting undergraduate and graduate student geospatial learning. The Geography & Environmental Science GA position is housed in the SDAV laboratory. 3. Marine Science: GA position provides support for MARE 171L and MARE 201L. Duties for MARE 171L and MARE 201L include, but are not limited to: 1) Shadowing 1 section of MARE 171L or MARE 201L (first semester) and independently teaching additional sections. 2) Collaborating/ working closely with the supervising instructor in Marine Science on course planning, preparation, grading, supervision of a lab assistant, and classroom management in the lab and in the field. 3) Ensuring the posting and grading of assignments is consistent with that of the supervising instructor, and return assignments promptly. 4) Holding office hours and conducting informal discussion groups/study sessions to ensure understanding of content and assignments. 5) Responding appropriately and professionally to students outside of class. 6) Supervising students in the laboratory as well as in the field. 7) Training and monitoring laboratory users (including in the field) for compliance with safety and scientific procedures. 8) Ensuring proper maintenance of laboratory equipment and supplies. 8) Transporting students in a 12- or 15-passenger van. 9) Performing other duties as assigned. Relevant coursework for MARE 171L: a 100-level or higher Marine Biology course and lab. Skills for MARE 171L: must be able to swim and snorkel. Relevant coursework for MARE 201L: 200-level or higher Oceanography course and lab. Skills for MARE 201L: experience working on a boat, handling lines on a boat. 4. College of Agriculture, Forestry, Natural Resources Management: GA position provides support for AG 230. Duties include, but are not limited to: 1) Maintaining assigned greenhouse benches for growing seedlings. 2) Maintaining an inventory of tools and equipment. 3) Keeping tools and equipment in good working condition. 4) Acquiring and preparing supplies for the class. 5) Collecting, grading, and providing feedback on the students’ weekly journal. 6) Teaching 1-2 classes on topics related to thesis or sustainability. 7) Co-supervising and providing technical assistance in the development of garden plots on campus or farm. 8) Substitute teaching the class when the main instructor is absent. 9) Providing assistance to another CAFNRM class, if needed. This position may also provide support to the TCBES Graduate Program. Possible tasks include maintaining and updating the TCBES website and social media accounts, assisting with TCBES vehicle maintenance, rentals, and payments, assisting with managing the TCBES workroom, and assisting with the TCBES Newsletter. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: 1. C over letter explaining how you satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, clearly expressing your skills and abilities in teaching, communicating, and working with others; 2. Curriculum Vitae or resume; 3. Names, telephone numbers and email addresses of three [3] professional references; 4. Transcript(s) (official transcripts will be required prior to hiring; unofficial transcripts are acceptable for your initial application. Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Pat Hart, Associate Director TCBES Graduate Program; pjhart@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5510800/instructional-and-student-support-specialist-0077866t-0078964t-0080908t": {
      "title": "Instructional and Student Support Specialist (0077866T, 0078964T, 0080908T)",
      "description": "Instructional and Student Support Specialist (0077866T, 0078964T, 0080908T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02161 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division Student Affairs - Upward Bound Opening Date 10/08/2026 Closing Date 11/7/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0077866T, 0078964T, 0080908T Description Title: Instructional and Student Support Specialist (North Program Coordinator, West Program Coordinator, Math & Science Program Coordinator) Position Number: 0077866T, 0078964T, 0080908T Hiring Unit: Student Affairs, Upward Bound Location: UH at Hilo Date Posted: October 8, 2026 Closing Date: November 7, 2026 Band: A Salary : salary schedules and placement information Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Position Overview The University of Hawai’i at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public liberal arts and sciences university on the Island of Hawai’i, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawai’i System, UH Hilo offers high-quality undergraduate and select postgraduate education, with distinctive strengths in areas such as marine science, geology, astronomy, and Hawaiian language revitalization, while advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity that support community needs, environmental stewardship, Indigenous knowledge systems, and workforce development for the State of Hawai’i. The UH Hilo Division of Student Affairs serves as the primary resource for student support, engagement, and development outside of the classroom. This division provides essential programs and services that foster student success, personal growth, and a vibrant campus life. Student Affairs is responsible for critical functions such as advising and counseling services, housing and residential life, student activities and organizations, wellness and health services, career development, and promoting an inclusive and supportive campus environment. The Upward Bound Program at the University of Hawai’i at Hilo is a federally funded TRIO program designed to support high school students from low-income families and/or those who are potential first-generation college students. The program provides academic instruction, advising, college and career exploration, and enrichment opportunities that prepare participants for successful entry into and completion of postsecondary education. Upward Bound works in partnership with Hawai’i Island high schools, families, and community organizations to provide year-round services including academic advising, tutoring, academic workshops, college tours, cultural enrichment activities, financial aid and FAFSA completion support, and college application assistance. The Instructor and Student Support Specialist (Upward Bound Program Coordinator) plays a key role in supporting the academic development and college readiness of program participants. This position provides advising, academic support services, and college-prep programming that promotes student success in high school and preparation for postsecondary education. The incumbent works closely with students, families, school personnel, faculty, and community partners to recruit program participants, deliver college preparatory services, assist students with financial aid awareness and FAFSA completion, monitor participant progress, coordinate program activities, and ensure compliance with federal TRIO program requirements. Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions): *Under the direction of leadership, provide programming, advising, and services to meet the college preparatory needs of County participants; for Math & Science Upward Bound, develop activities tailored to STEM interests and postsecondary pathways leading to math and science degrees. *Recruit and enroll eligible applicants by evaluating applications, conducting interviews, and administering standardized assessments to evaluate academic need and potential for postsecondary success. *Use grades, test scores, and participant self-reports to develop and update individualized postsecondary education preparatory plans and admission recommendations aligned with student needs and program goals. *Coordinate and provide academic support services that meet individual participant needs, including academic advising, tutoring, supplemental instruction, study skills development, and test-taking strategies. *Provide direct assistance in applying to postsecondary institutions, including completing the FAFSA, distributing fee waivers, reviewing résumés/personal statements, and preparing letters of recommendation. *Monitor, document, and enter accurate participant services and progress data into tracking systems by established deadlines; maintain complete, organized files in accordance with program, university, and federal TRIO requirements (APR). *Plan, coordinate, and participate in program-related activities and events; supervise and ensure the safety of participants during all programming, including evenings, weekends, and field trips. Conduct in-person and virtual recruitment outreach at target schools and community events; deliver presentations explaining the mission and services to students, parents, and school personnel. Meet with participants regularly through in-person and virtual advising sessions to monitor academic progress, provide college and career readiness support, and address individual needs. Conduct exploration activities, including college fairs, tours, and STEM career shadowing opportunities; collaborate with faculty, mentors, and community partners to coordinate pre-college services. Participate in the recruitment, training, and supervision of seasonal staff and student assistants; contribute to the planning, evaluation, and creation of program materials and recruitment resources. Track and document Upward Bound alumni postsecondary admission, enrollment, and persistence toward graduation to support federal reporting; perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Education and Professional Work Experience: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in any field or related field and 0 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for providing Secondary or PS education advising; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of college access and student support services demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply concepts, terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of principles, theories, federal and state laws, rules, regulations and systems associated with secondary and postsecondary education systems. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve the full range problems that may commonly occur in the area of academic advising, student engagement, and educational program delivery. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software. Supplemental Minimum Qualifications: Possession of a valid driver’s license, safe driving record, and ability to travel to target schools and transport students for approved program activities. Ability to adjust quickly to change and effectively handle varied and challenging conditions, assignments, and priorities. Ability to work a flexible schedule, including evenings, weekends, and overnight travel or program events and college tours as required. Desirable Qualifications: One (1) year of professional experience providing college access, academic advising, student support services, youth development programming, or related educational services to secondary or postsecondary students. Successful experience working collaboratively with university faculty, secondary education personnel, and other professionals to develop and supplement program resources. Preference will be given to applicants who have overcome barriers similar to those confronting the low-income, first-generation college-bound population served by Upward Bound, consistent with 34 CFR Part 645.31(e)(3), such as being raised in a low-income or isolated community, attending an under-resourced high school, or experiencing circumstances that limited access to college-going opportunities. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: Cover letter explaining how each minimum and desirable qualification is met. Cover letter should include which position you are interested in (North Program Coordinator, West Program Coordinator, Math & Science Program Coordinator) Resume Names and addresses, email addresses and telephone numbers of three [3] current professional references Transcript(s) showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment). Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Sarah Lips, slips@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5358777/instructor-administration-of-justice-pos0087561": {
      "title": "Instructor (Administration of Justice) (pos#0087561)",
      "description": "Instructor (Administration of Justice) (pos#0087561) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01128 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division College of Arts and Sciences - Social Sciences Division Opening Date 06/23/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0087561 Description Title: Instructor (Administration of Justice) (UPDATED 8/14/26) Position Number: 0087561 Hiring Unit: College of Arts & Sciences/Social Sciences Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: June 23, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment, application review begins on or about October 1, 2026, and will continue until position is filled Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 9-month Tenure Track: Non-tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Anticipated start date is January or August 2027, pending position clearance and availability of funding. Position Overview The University of Hawaii at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public liberal arts and sciences university on the Island of Hawaii, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island’s extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawaii system, UH Hilo offers high-quality undergraduate and select postgraduate education, with distinctive strengths in areas such as marine science, geology, astronomy, and Hawaiian language revitalization, while advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity that support community needs, environmental stewardship, Indigenous knowledge systems, and workforce development priorities for the State of Hawaii. The College of Arts and Sciences offers Bachelor of Arts (BA) degrees, Bachelor of Science (BS) degrees, Master's degrees, and specialized undergraduate certificate programs. The College also offers Master's degree programs in Education, Counseling Psychology and Heritage Management. Many of these programs take special advantage of our island location. The Department of Administration of Justice at University of Hawaii at Hilo invites applications for the position of Instructor of Administration of Justice. This Instructor position is responsible for teaching a wide range of AJ courses in the area of criminal justice each semester based on department needs as well as assisting with student events and co-curricular activities in the AJ department. The Department of Administration of Justice offers a BA in Administration of Justice (AJ) that includes a fully online Law Enforcement Cohort Track specifically designed for law enforcement officers, and the department also offers a Criminal Justice (CJ) certificate. The mission of the Department of Administration of Justice is to prepare students to be innovative leaders in law enforcement, courts and corrections, criminal justice administration, homeland security, and justice advocacy; qualify our majors for acceptance to top-notch graduate programs and law schools; and support the community through engaged scholarship and service. Additional information about the Department of Administration of Justice can be found at https://hilo.hawaii.edu/depts/justice/ . Duties and Responsibilities Teach undergraduate courses, including Intro to Administration of Justice, Crime and Society, Criminal Justice, Policing and Punishment, Criminal Law and Procedure, Public Policy Analysis, Advocacy and Criminal Justice, Public Leadership & Ethics, and other upper division AJ courses as needed. Hold weekly on-campus office hours. Assist with student events and co-curricular activities. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or closely related social science discipline. Demonstrated expertise in the field of criminal justice. Successful teaching at the university level in both online and face-to-face instruction. Desirable Qualifications Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or closely related social science discipline. A proven track record of effective course instruction in lower and upper-division criminal justice courses at the university level. Experience teaching in a multi-cultural setting. Applied experience in law enforcement. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: Cover letter addressing ability to perform stated duties and specifically explaining how minimum and desirable qualifications are met Current curriculum vita Statement of teaching philosophy Course evaluations and sample syllabi A list of three (3) current references, including mailing address, e-mail, phone number(s) (letters or recommendation may be requested after initial review) Academic transcripts (copies of unofficial transcripts are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment). Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach required documents. Applicant must submit the following: Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Katherine Young, youngkat@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5476997/instructor-in-nursing-0084204": {
      "title": "Instructor in Nursing (0084204)",
      "description": "Instructor in Nursing (0084204) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-01948 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division College of Natural and Health Sciences - School of Nursing Opening Date 09/09/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0084204 Description Title: Instructor in Nursing Position Number: 84204 Hiring Unit: College of Natural & Health Sciences (CNHS), School of Nursing Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: 09/09/2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment, first review on or about October 15, 2026, and will continue until position is filled. Salary Information: Commensurate with qualification and experience Monthly Type: 9-month Tenure-Track: Non-Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: Anticipated start date is January 2027, pending position clearance and availability of funding. Position Overview The University of Hawai'i at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public liberal arts and sciences university on the Island of Hawai'i, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawai'i System, UH Hilo offers high-quality undergraduate and select postgraduate education, with distinctive strengths in areas such as marine science, geology, astronomy, and Hawaiian language revitalization, while advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity that support community needs, environmental stewardship, Indigenous knowledge systems, and workforce development for the State of Hawai'i. The College of Natural and Health Sciences is one of the two largest colleges at UH Hilo, home to 9 departments, 14 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, and over 60 full-time faculty. CNHS provides an innovative, career-focused scientific education based in the culture, rich diversity, and natural environment of Hawai`i. The college prepares students for success in local, state, regional, and global careers. It teaches basic scientific and mathematical principles and applications that inform future citizens, scientists, managers, and leaders using modern methods and Hawai`i Island's living laboratory. The School of Nursing (SON) at the University of Hawai'i at Hilo (UH Hilo) invites applications for a non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Instructor. The responsibilities of this position are in instructional and programmatic activities only, with no requirement of a research component. The UH School of Nursing offers a bachelor of science degree in nursing (BSN) and a doctor of nursing practice (DNP). The program provides a learning environment that is responsive to the needs of a diverse student population and involves a rigorous, high-quality education in a caring, personalized atmosphere. This educational experience encourages student-faculty interactions and offers hands-on learning and leadership opportunities. The nursing program emphasizes life-long learning and teaches students to deliver culturally congruent nursing care in a rural environment. More information about the School of Nursing can be found at https://hilo.hawaii.edu/depts/nursing/ Duties and Responsibilities : Teach the equivalent of 12 credits of courses per semester in the undergraduate Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN), RN to BSN, and/or Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) programs. Teaching duties include in-person classroom lectures, in-person clinical practicum and labs, and courses delivered by distance education. Faculty are expected to be available to teach in-person on Hawai‘i Island throughout the week. Instructional days during the week will depend on the courses being taught. Advise students through their degree pathway. Engage in development of outcomes assessment. Participate in faculty meetings and mandatory in-person weekly office hours per university policy. Minimum qualifications: Master’s or higher degree in nursing. Eligible for Hawaii RN licensure and other established professional and health requirements. Acute care experience in medical/surgical, obstetrical, mental health, pediatric, and/or long term health care. Experience in teaching or working with undergraduate clinical nursing students in acute care, long term care, mental health, obstetrics and/or pediatrics settings, or a combination of education and experience commensurate with the requirements of this position. Excellent communication, planning, and organizational skills as demonstrated by the application materials. Desirable qualifications: Doctorate in nursing or a nursing-related field. Experience teaching on Hawai‘i Island or with culturally diverse student populations in rural clinical settings. Extensive experience with acute care adult health clinical instruction in BSN nursing programs. Experience with asynchronous education technology and outcomes assessment. Experience coordinating clinical programs in undergraduate baccalaureate nursing programs. Background or experience with simulated learning experiences, including the use of high-fidelity patient simulators via detailed, clinically-based scenarios with instruction, evaluation, and remediation in didactic and/or clinical learning settings. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: 1) A cover letter clearly addressing how each minimum and desirable qualification is met as applicable and why you are interested in this position. Please also indicate any of the listed desirable qualifications you possess. 2) A current curriculum vita that clearly lists all past work experience and the specific names and delineation of undergraduate versus graduate levels of courses that have been taught in the past. 3) A statement of teaching philosophy including evidence of high-quality teaching. 4) Three (3) current professional references (including names, affiliations, addresses, e-mail addresses, and telephone numbers). 5) Academic transcripts for all undergraduate and graduate nursing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, official transcripts are required prior to employment). 6) Copy of current unencumbered registered nurse license (and advanced practice registered nurse license if applicable). An unencumbered Hawai‘i registered nurse license (and advanced practice registered nurse license if applicable) will be required prior to employment. Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries Dr. Jeanette Ayers-Kawakami; ayersjea@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
