    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5517307/associate-dean-academic-affairs-pos-no-0089468": {
      "title": "Associate Dean (Academic Affairs) (Pos No 0089468)",
      "description": "Associate Dean (Academic Affairs) (Pos No 0089468) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02210 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo - College of Pharmacy Division Dean's Office Opening Date 10/14/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0089468 Description Title: Associate Dean, Academic Affairs (UPDATED 10/15/26) Position Number: 0089468 Hiring Unit: Daniel K. Inouye College of Pharmacy Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: October 14, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment. First review on or about January 5, 2027 and will continue until the position is filled Salary Range: $129,048 to $232,293 Annual (10/15/26 correction to salary range) Additional Salary Info: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Funding: General Funds Other Conditions: Appointment to begin February 2027, pending position clearance and availability of funding. Position Overview The University of Hawaii at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public university on the Island of Hawaii, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawaii System, UH Hilo offers undergraduate, graduate, and professional degrees than span six colleges: Arts & Sciences; Natural & Health Sciences; Business Administration; Agriculture, Forestry & Natural Resources; Hawaiian Language; and Pharmacy. The colleges are accountable to the Office of the Vice Chancellor for Academic Affairs at UH Hilo, which provides leadership and administrative oversight for the instructional, research, and academic support units of the campus. The Daniel K. Inouye College of Pharmacy (DKICP) is the only pharmacy school in Hawaii and the Pacific Basin. The college is committed to offering quality heath professional education to meet the needs of the state; driving innovations that advance health, science and technology; and providing outreach and services that empower health and well-being in communities. This executive management position reports directly to the Dean of the DKICP and is responsible for providing administrative leadership that supports the Dean in advancing the academic, student, and administrative affairs of the college. The position is based in Hilo, Hawaii. INTRODUCTION This position reports to the Dean of the Daniel K. Inouye College of Pharmacy (DKICP) and provides leadership, administration and coordination for the Academic Programs in the College. Responsibilities encompass curriculum development and evaluation, human resources management, resource development, financial and academic planning, faculty development, maintenance of the Accreditation Council for Pharmacy Education (ACPE) accreditation, continuing education/professional development and post-graduate training, and development of curricular and programmatic enhancements and research. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Develop, implement, maintain and revise the curriculum for academic degree programs in the DKICP in partnership with the Curriculum Committee and Assessment Committee and respective directors of academic programs. Facilitate the development and adoption of best and promising curricular, instructional delivery, and clinical practices. Collaborate with Department Chairs and Program Directors to recruit, mentor, retain, and evaluate faculty of the academic programs in accord to UHH and accreditation standards, policies and procedures. Secure resources for curricular/instructional, student and faculty support for all academic programs. Create and sustain an environment that promotes, but is not limited to, fostering morale, academic vigor, instructional excellence, and research productivity. Provide leadership to meet the mission and advance the vision of the College of Pharmacy as it relates to academic affairs. Oversee and manage the ACPE accreditation process for the PharmD program and any other accreditation processes for other academic degree programs. Conduct scholarly work and maintain professional activities to account for pharmacy scholarship, including the scholarship of teaching and learning. Provide leadership and support for initiatives that foster the dissemination of knowledge and the creation of new knowledge among faculty, staff and students. Oversee priorities in academic affairs for the College that include, but are not limited to, securing ACPE accreditation; recruitment, admission, assessment and graduation and promotion of professional students; hiring and retention of faculty; management of staff support personnel and resources; other relevant activities to the academic offerings of the College. Oversee and manage the process for assigning and training advisors (Academic, Student Organization) in coordination with the Dean’s Executive Committee. Manage data, training and processes to support student progression and graduation. Assure, in conjunction with the Department of Pharmacy Practice Chair and Director of Experiential Education, that service and community fairs are properly staffed with faculty and/or registered pharmacists and that appropriate quality controls are enforced. Assure a process for approval of holding community health fairs, staffing and follow up. Assure that professional student organizations are compliant with chapter rules and regulations, organizations are dynamic, and student needs are met. Collaborate with DKICP and university personnel involved in recruitment and admissions and facilitate the coordination of college resources to meet enrollment targets. Produce budgetary plans and fiscal documents in accountability to fiscal integrity of the academic programs in the DKICP. Secure fiscal and institutional support for the PharmD program via grant-writing, collaborate with the Dean in fundraising, and participate in the State of Hawaii legislative hearings. Collaborate with directors in the college (eg. IPE, experiential education, distance education, recruitment and admissions) and university staff (eg. academic affairs, financial aid, registrar office, student affairs) to advance the academic mission and student progression. Communicate with academic leaders and represent the College of Pharmacy as needed at UH System Graduate Program forums, the UHH Vice Chancellor of Academic Leadership Team, UHH Graduate Council and other ACPE/AACP Graduate Program national forums. Lead assessment efforts in the college to ensure the quality of the curriculum, compliance with ACPE standards, and CQI for the strategic plan. Adhere to applicable laws, regulations, and policies and procedures of the University, raise awareness among staff, and direct them in such a way that University compliance requirements are met. Identify and mitigate through policy development/revision/enhancement and training, areas of risk and developing risk to the University, e.g., legal, financial, reputation, and compliance risk, and ensure team is aware of addressing same. Other duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS An earned doctorate - PhD or professional doctorate (PharmD, MD, DO) Three (3) years of relevant academic or administrative experience. Attained the academic rank of associate professor or have demonstrated a record of comparable professional experience prior to appointment. Leadership experience with successful accreditation of an academic program. Experience making data driven decisions. Ability to comprehend and interpret administrative directives and guidance to ensure IT operations align with organizational standard and industry best practices. DESIRABLE QUALIFICATIONS Five (5) years of administrative experience with progressive responsibilities in a U.S. College or School of Pharmacy. Documentation of teaching excellence in graduate/professional programs at a U.S. academic institution. Demonstrated record of curriculum development and evaluation leadership in graduate/professional programs at a U.S. College or School of Pharmacy or related basic or health science. Attainment of the academic rank of full professor. Health professional license or eligibility for licensure in Hawaii. A track record of peer-reviewed research funding. Scholarship documented by a publication track record in peer-reviewed journals, presentations at national and international forums. Experience in fundraising and external relations To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: Cover letter highlighting interest in the position, the ability to perform the stated duties, how the applicant meets all minimum qualifications and any desirable qualifications, and how these experiences would help advance academic, student and administrative affairs in the college. Current curriculum vitae. One-to-two page statement of leadership philosophy, including approach to fostering excellence, innovation, accountability, and collaboration among faculty, staff, students, and other existing and potential partners. Academic transcripts showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, official transcripts are required prior to employment); The names and addresses, email addresses and telephone numbers of three [3] current professional references. Contact information (names, affiliations, addresses, e-mail addresses, and telephone numbers) of three current professional references. Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Simon Kattenhorn, simonkat@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5516975/information-technology-specialist-machine-learningpos0097629t": {
      "title": "Information Technology Specialist (Machine Learning)(pos#0097629T)",
      "description": "Information Technology Specialist (Machine Learning)(pos#0097629T) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Part-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-02202 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division College of Arts and Sciences - Geography Opening Date 10/14/2026 Closing Date 10/28/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0097629T Description Title: Information Technology Specialist (Machine Learning) Position Number: 0097629T Hiring Unit: College of Arts & Sciences, Geography Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: October 13, 2026 Closing Date: October 28, 2026 Band: A Salary: salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBA, minimum $4627/month Full Time/Part Time: Part-time (20 hrs/week) Temporary/Permanent: Temporary Other Conditions: Appointment to begin approximately November 2026, pending position clearance and availability of funding. Position Overview The University of Hawaii at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public liberal arts and sciences university on the Island of Hawaii, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawaii system, UH Hilo offers high-quality undergraduate and select postgraduate education, with distinctive strengths in areas such as marine science, geology, astronomy, and Hawaiian language revitalization, while advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity that support community needs, environmental stewardship, Indigenous knowledge systems, and workforce development priorities for the State of Hawaii. The mission of the Spatial Data Analysis and Visualization (SDAV) research lab at UH Hilo is to apply geospatial tools to environmental problems of local significance, and to disseminate geospatial tools and knowledge to the larger island community through education and outreach activities. Our lab utilizes a suite of geospatial technologies such as small unmanned aerial system (sUAS) platforms, LiDAR, and hyperspectral imaging, to support ongoing research and educational projects. The Information Technology Specialist (Machine Learning) position within the UH Hilo SDAV Research laboratory is responsible for maintaining and updating the code and computer hardware and software required to carry out our research tasks relating to machine learning. Duties and Responsibilities (*denotes essential functions): *Follows and implements directives and guidance related to best practices from University of Hawaii System Information Technology Services. *Ensures the consistent adoption, implementation, and enforcement of recommendations issued through University of Hawaii System Information Technology Service. *Keeps abreast of recommendations issued through University of Hawaii System Information Technology Service, and takes timely action as needed. Provides staff support in the following field of assignment or subject matter area: Machine Learning. Supports senior associates in the performance of specific work assignments, such as maintaining SDAV research lab's machine learning (ML) code base, training and retraining ML models as needed for research project goals, and performing statistical analysis to improve ML models. Plans, coordinates, analyzes, and/or supports work within the subject matter area of ML. Works with supervisor and subject matter specialists to ensure accuracy and adequacy of work. Serves as a source of support for completion of projects in/of/related to ML and object detection. Maintains, evaluates, tests, operates, calibrates, and/or develops equipment or components, e.g., ML-related hardware and software. Installs, tests, documents, and maintains systems and network hardware, including firmware upgrades. Assists with backup and recovery procedures. Installs, tests, and maintains software products and upgrades. Assists when problems occur with applications and hardware. Performs technical problem diagnostics and problem resolution with the assistance of systems administrators, developers, application change-management administrators, and vendor technical support as necessary. Provides hardware support such as installation of network cards, and reformatting of hard disk drives when required. Keeps current on rapid developments and emerging technologies by researching articles in journals, periodicals, technical manuals, & by attending classes, conferences, presentations and self-directed study methods. Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications Possession of a baccalaureate educational degree in Data Science or related field and 2 year(s) of progressively responsible professional information technology experience with responsibilities for machine learning; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Functional knowledge of machine learning and coding demonstrated by knowledge, understanding and ability to apply principles, concepts, methodology and terminology. Functional knowledge and understanding of technology, systems, hardware and software associated with Python, Linux, CUDA, Conda, Git, WSL, Tensor, Pytorch, Slurm, Windows 10/11, Synology NAS, PC hardware installation, and basic networking. Demonstrated ability to recognize problems, identify possible causes and resolve routine problems in the area of machine learning. Ability to comprehend, interpret and implement administrative directives and guidance to ensure IT operations align with organizational standards and industry best practices. Demonstrated ability to understand oral and written documentation, write reports and procedures, and communicate effectively in a variety of situations. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team members and individuals. Ability to lift and carry up to thirty (30) pounds unassisted. Desirable Qualifications Experience working with conservation projects in Hawaii. Experience with Deep Forest python package and YOLO. Experience identifying invasive and native Hawaiian flora and fauna, and identifying symptoms associated with Rapid Ohia Death. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: Cover letter explaining how each minimum and desirable qualification is met. Current resume. The names and addresses, email addresses and telephone numbers of three [3] current professional references. Transcript(s) showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment). Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Ryan Perroy, rperroy@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5516915/institutional-policy-analyst-confidential-advocate-prevention-educator-pos00": {
      "title": "Institutional/Policy Analyst (Confidential Advocate & Prevention Educator) pos#0080445",
      "description": "Institutional/Policy Analyst (Confidential Advocate & Prevention Educator) pos#0080445 Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02201 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division Office of Equal Opportunity Opening Date 10/14/2026 Closing Date 11/4/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0080445 Description Title: Institutional/Policy Analyst (Confidential Advocate & Prevention Educator) Position Number: 0080445 Hiring Unit: Office of Equal Opportunity (OEO) Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: October 14, 2026 Closing Date: November 4, 2026 Band: A or B Salary: salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information:PBA, minimum $4627/month; PBB, minimum $5640/month Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: Appointment to begin approximately November 2026, pending position clearance and availability of funding. Position Overview The University of Hawaii at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public liberal arts and sciences university on the Island of Hawaii, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawaii system, UH Hilo offers high-quality undergraduate and select postgraduate education, with distinctive strengths in areas such as marine science, geology, astronomy, and Hawaiian language revitalization, while advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity that support community needs, environmental stewardship, Indigenous knowledge systems, and workforce development priorities for the State of Hawaii. At UH Hilo, we seek to cultivate an inclusive community of people with varied characteristics, ideas, cultures, and world-views where our students, faculty, and staff celebrate difference and respect tradition. To aid in these efforts, it is the policy and commitment of the University of Hawaii to ensure equal opportunity in higher education, both as a provider of education and as an employer. The Office of Equal Opportunity drives UH Hilo's efforts to stop, prevent and remedy discrimination, while seeking to ensure a diverse, equitable and inclusive work and learning environment. The Confidential Advocate & Prevention Educator is responsible for providing confidential support to UH Hilo students who may have experienced sex or gender-based discrimination and to create and implement sex and gender-based discrimination prevention educational programming for the UH Hilo campus community. Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions): *Serve as a confidential student resource for concerns related to sex and gender-based violence and misconduct. Informs students of options to address sex and gender-based discrimination under UH Executive Policy 1.204 and Administrative Procedures 1.204. *Provide crisis response, advocacy, and referrals to student victims of all forms of sex and gender-based violence, including, but not limited to, sexual assault, interpersonal violence, staling, sexual harassment or other forms of sexual misconduct. *Support and advocate for students who may have experienced sex or gender-based discrimination throughout the various stages of reporting and adjudicating complaints. *Maximize student safety, retention and success through policy and prevention education and programming. *Design or obtain and implement culturally competent, intersectional prevention and bystander intervention curriculum. *Lead and support student assistants with the implementation of prevention education programming. *Assess students' needs and work with faculty, advisors, on campus employers, etc. to identify and implement interim measures and/or support services that ensure equal access to the educational environment for students who have experienced sex or gender-based violence. *Ensure that University, State and Federal processes and standards are upheld. *Collect data and report on aggregate data in order to identify trends, and plan for future education and outreach efforts. Evaluate impact of education efforts on campus climate. Develop and maintain working relationships with campus partners, community stakeholders and across the UH System. Participate in mandatory training on a regular basis as identified by UH leadership. Recommend best practices for policies and procedures pertaining to victim services. Serves as a resource to administration, faculty and staff as it pertains to the University's responsibilities regarding prevention and response to sexual and gender-based discrimination. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Band A: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Social Work, Sociology, Psychology, Education, Criminal Justice, Public Health or related field and 1 year of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for crisis support, outreach, case management and/or training; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Band B: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Social Work, Sociology, Psychology, Education, Criminal Justice, Public Health or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for crisis support, outreach, case management and/or training; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of sex and gender-based discrimination as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software. For supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Ability to identify and respond to the needs of students who report experiencing sex or gender-based discrimination. Demonstrated ability to work independently, manage time and create a personalized and confidential record keeping system. Demonstrated ability to maintain confidential information. Desirable Qualifications Functional knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in victim advocacy, university programming or education.Experience working in a college setting.Experience using UH Hilo data tracking systems such as STAR.Demonstrated record of working with diverse populations.Familiarity with equal opportunity principles. Familiar with University of Hawai'i policies and procedures on sex and gender-based discrimination. Experience implementing prevention education programs. Awareness of prevention education and victim advocacy best practices in a university setting. Possession of a Master's Degree in Social Work, Counseling, Psychology, Sociology, Education or related field. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: Cover letter explaining how each minimum and desirable qualification is met. Current resume. The names and addresses, email addresses and telephone numbers of three [3] current professional references. Transcript(s) showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment). Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Shaunda Makaimoku, shaunda@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
