    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5145184/asst-prof-of-nursing-0085836-0085839-0085860-updated-8-1-26": {
      "title": "Asst Prof of Nursing (0085836, 0085839, 0085860) (UPDATED 8/1/26)",
      "description": "Asst Prof of Nursing (0085836, 0085839, 0085860) (UPDATED 8/1/26) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2025-02402 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division College of Natural and Health Sciences - School of Nursing Opening Date 11/17/2025 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0085836, 0085837, 0085839, 0085860 Description Title: Assistant Professor of Nursing Position Numbers: 0085836, 0085839, 0085860 Hiring Unit: College of Natural & Health Sciences (CNHS)/School of Nursing Location: UH-Hilo Date Posted: 11/17/2025 Closing Date: Continuous - application review begins February 2, 2026 Salary Information: Commensurate with qualification and experience Monthly Type: 9-month Tenure-Track: Tenure Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: Anticipated start date is August 2026, pending position clearance and availability of funding. The School of Nursing (SON) at the University of Hawai'i at Hilo (UH Hilo) invites applications for four (4) tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor. The responsibilities of this position include instructional, research, and service components. The UH School of Nursing offers a bachelor of science degree in nursing (BSN) and a doctor of nursing practice (DNP). The program provides a learning environment that is responsive to the needs of a diverse student population and involves a rigorous, high-quality education in a caring, personalized atmosphere. This educational experience encourages student-faculty interactions and offers hands-on learning and leadership opportunities. The nursing program emphasizes life-long learning and teaches students to deliver culturally congruent nursing care in a rural environment. More information about the School of Nursing can be found at https://hilo.hawaii.edu/depts/nursing/ Duties and Responsibilities: Teaching courses in the Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN), RN to BSN, and/or Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) programs Duties include teaching in-person classroom lectures, in-person clinical practicum and lab courses, and courses delivered by distance education. Faculty are expected to be available to teach in-person on Hawai‘i Island throughout the week. Instructional days during the week will depend on the courses being taught. Advise students through their degree pathway Engage in scholarly activity Assist in development of outcomes assessment Participate in faculty meetings and mandatory in-person weekly office hours per university policy. Participate in University, departmental, and community service; serve on SON committees including accreditation work and community events. Minimum qualifications: Doctorate in a nursing-related field. Must be eligible for Hawai‘i RN licensure and other established professional and health requirements. Experience in teaching or working with undergraduate and/or graduate clinical nursing students in acute care, medical/surgical , long-term care, mental health, obstetrics and/or pediatrics settings. A combination of education and experience commensurate with the requirements of this position may be considered for candidates without formal teaching experience. Excellent communication, planning, and organizational skills. Desirable qualifications: Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) degree. Experience teaching on Hawai‘i Island with culturally diverse student populations in rural clinical settings. Eligible for Hawaii APRN FNP licensure. Clinical experience in acute care, long-term care, mental health, primary care, and/or general medical/surgical practice. Experience with synchronous and asynchronous education technology and outcomes assessment. Administration of grants and research experience. Experience with instruction in BSN and/or DNP nursing program and doctoral projects. Experience coordinating clinical programs in undergraduate Baccalaureate nursing programs. Background or experience with simulated learning experiences, including the use of high-fidelity patient simulators via detailed, clinically-based scenarios with instruction, evaluation, and remediation in didactic and/or clinical learning settings. To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each of the required document. A complete application must include the following: A cover letter clearly addressing how each minimum and desirable qualification is met as applicable and why you are interested in this position. Please also indicate any of the listed desirable qualifications you possess. A current curriculum vita that clearly lists all past work experience and the specific names and delineation of undergraduate versus graduate levels of courses that have been taught in the past, as well as past projects and industry/research/fundraising experience. A statement of teaching philosophy including evidence of high-quality teaching. Three (3) current professional references (including names, affiliations, addresses, e-mail addresses, and telephone numbers). Transcript(s) showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment). Transcripts issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. Copies of diplomas will not be accepted. Copy of current unencumbered registered nurse license (and advanced practice registered nurse license if applicable). An unencumbered Hawaii registered nurse license (and advanced practice registered nurse license if applicable) will be required prior to employment. Please do not include any self-identifying photos they will be redacted. PLEASE REDACT REFERENCES TO SOCIAL SECURITY NUMBERS AND BIRTH DATE ON TRANSCRIPT COPIES. Copies of diplomas will NOT be accepted. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Patricia Hensley; hensleyp@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5531074/student-activities-officer-associate-director-of-campus-center-pos-0078142": {
      "title": "Student Activities Officer (Associate Director of Campus Center) (pos #0078142)",
      "description": "Student Activities Officer (Associate Director of Campus Center) (pos #0078142) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02298 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Division Student Affairs - Student Activities Opening Date 10/27/2026 Closing Date 11/13/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0078142 Description Title: Student Activities Officer (Associate Director of Campus Center) Position Number: 0078142 Hiring Unit: Student Affairs, Student Activities Location: UH at Hilo Date Posted: October 27, 2026 Closing Date: November 13, 2026 Band: B Salary : salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information: PBB, minimum $5,640/month Full Time/Part Time: Full-time Month: 11-month Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: Appointment to begin approximately December 2026, pending position clearance and availability of funding. Note: To ensure a consistent digital layout across third-party software platforms, Hawaiian language (kahako and okina) and other such diacritical marks have been omitted from this online posting. The University recognizes and respects the proper use of these markings in all official communications. Position Overview: The University of Hawaii at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public liberal arts and sciences university on the Island of Hawaii, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawaii System, UH Hilo offers high-quality undergraduate and select postgraduate education, with distinctive strengths in areas such as marine science, geology, astronomy, and Hawaiian language revitalization, while advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity that support community needs, environmental stewardship, Indigenous knowledge systems, and workforce development for the State of Hawaii. The UH Hilo Division of Student Affairs serves as the primary resource for student support, engagement, and development outside of the classroom. This division provides essential programs and services that foster student success, personal growth, and a vibrant campus life. Student Affairs is responsible for critical functions such as advising and counseling services, housing and residential life, student activities and organizations, wellness and health services, career development, and promoting an inclusive and supportive campus environment. The Associate Director for the Campus Center provides leadership, planning, and administrative oversight for campus center programs and services, student organization advising, student leadership development, co-curricular engagement, and student-centered programming operations. The position directs student involvement initiatives, leadership training programs, and campus engagement efforts that enhance student learning and community building outside the classroom, while supporting operations within a comprehensive student engagement facility and supervising professional and student staff responsible for campus programming, media, and co-curricular services. Duties and Responsibilities (* denotes essential functions): Leadership and Administration 1. *Provide leadership and strategic direction for Campus Center programs, services, and initiatives that support student involvement, leadership development, co-curricular engagement, belonging, and student success. 2. *Provide managerial and administrative oversight of Campus Center operations, programs, and services; establish priorities, performance expectations, and accountability standards to ensure effective and sustainable operations. 3. *Supervise and evaluate professional staff and student employees, providing direction for recruitment, onboarding, training, performance management, professional development, and team effectiveness. 4. *Develop, manage, and monitor Campus Center budgets, exercise delegated fiscal authority, and ensure responsible use of resources in alignment with program priorities and institutional requirements. 5. *Evaluate Campus Center programs, services, and operations through assessment, data, reports, and feedback; identify opportunities for improvement and recommend organizational or programmatic changes that support strategic goals. 6. *Assist the Director with operational planning and reporting, including annual summaries, program reviews, assessments, strategic planning, and other reports related to Campus Center activities and outcomes. Student Organizations and Leadership Development 1. *Provide strategic leadership and oversight for Chartered Student Organizations (CSOs) and Board of Regents-chartered and registered independent organizations; serve as a primary University liaison, interpret and administer applicable BOR and University policies and procedures, and support organizational development, governance, compliance, and effective operations. 2. *Advise and develop student leaders in governance, organizational effectiveness, strategic planning, team development, event planning, conflict resolution, and other areas that strengthen student leadership and organizational capacity. 3. *Develop, implement, and assess student leadership education and training, identifying student development needs and providing workshops, programs, and learning experiences that promote leadership, collaboration, and engagement. Student Engagement and Campus Involvement 1. *Develop and oversee student involvement, volunteer, and engagement initiatives that create opportunities for students to participate, build community, develop leadership skills, and connect with the campus. 2. *Establish and implement policies, procedures, and recruitment strategies that support student participation, organizational involvement, volunteer engagement, and effective delivery of Campus Center programs. 3. *Collaborate with students, staff, and campus partners to develop and promote co-curricular programs, activities, and engagement opportunities that support student success and a vibrant campus community. 4. *Oversee Campus Center engagement communications and recognition efforts, including digital platforms, social media, marketing and promotional materials, public relations, and initiatives that recognize student participation and leadership. Other 1. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: 1. Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Arts and Science, Business and Economics, Natural and Health Sciences, Higher Education, Social Work, or Student Affairs or related field and 3 year(s) of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for planning, directing, and evaluating student life development, student engagement programs, student organization services, and co-curricular operations; including two (2) years of supervisor experience or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. 2. Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices and techniques in the area of student life as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles and methodologies. 3. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations and theories and systems associated with student life. 4. Demonstrated ability to resolve wide ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. 5. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally and by preparation of reports and other materials. 6. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. 7. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer, apply word processing software, spreadsheet, and presentation software. 8. If applicable, for supervisory work, demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. 9. Considerable knowledge of the principles and practices of collegiate student development, co-curricular student leadership development, Student Engagement, and Student Development. Supplement Minimum Qualifications: 1. Ability to develop, implement, and evaluate student leadership development programs, campus events, or co-curricular engagement initiatives. 2. Considerable knowledge of mandatory student fee structures and their impact on fiscal management, program development, and organizational sustainability. 3. Experience advising student fee-funded programs, student government, and Chartered Student Organizations (CSOs), including guidance on appropriate use of funds and compliance with University of Hawai'i policies and procedures. 4. Considerable knowledge of the principles of facility management. 5. Considerable knowledge of the principles of personnel management related to recruitment, selection, and training. 6. Experience managing campus center or student engagement facilities, including event coordination and facility utilization. 7. Demonstrated ability to identify, develop, and nurture community and campus partnerships that enhance student engagement, strengthen campus life initiatives, and build post-UH Hilo connections. Desirable Qualifications 1. Master's degree in Arts and Science, Business and Economics, Natural and Health Sciences, Higher Education, Social Work, or Student Affairs, or related field. 2. Five (5) years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for planning, directing, and evaluating student life development, student engagement programs, student organization services, and co-curricular operations. 3. Three (3) years of managing and supervising student life, student engagement, or campus programming operations in a higher education setting. 4. Experience leading a comprehensive student union/student life program and operation. 5. Experience managing student programming budgets, student fee funds, and operational budgets, including financial planning and forecasting. 6. Demonstrated leadership in organizational change or restructuring. 7. Experience developing and implementing student leadership development programs, campus events, or co-curricular engagement initiatives. To Apply: Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each required document. A complete application must include: 1. Cover letter explaining how each minimum and desirable qualification is met. 2. Current resume. 3. The names and addresses, email addresses and telephone numbers of three [3] current professional reference 4. Transcript(s) showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, original official transcripts will be required prior to employment). Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Lai Sha Bugado, sdelo@hawaii.edu , (808) 932-7365 EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo Address 200 W. Kawili Street Hilo, Hawaii, 96720 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5535054/clinical-assistant-professor-pharmacy-practice-am-care-0073370": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor – Pharmacy Practice (Am Care) (0073370)",
      "description": "Clinical Assistant Professor – Pharmacy Practice (Am Care) (0073370) Salary See Position Description Location UH Hilo, Hilo, HI Job Type Full-Time Permanent Job Number 2026-02316 Department University of Hawai'i at Hilo - College of Pharmacy Division Pharmacy Practice Opening Date 10/29/2026 Closing Date Continuous Position Number: 0073370 Description Title: Clinical Assistant Professor – Pharmacy Practice (Am Care) Hiring Unit: Daniel K Inouye College of Pharmacy Position: 73370 Location: UH Hilo Date Posted: October 29, 2026 Closing Date: Continuous recruitment, first review on or about January 5, 2027 and will continue until the position is filled Salary: Commensurate with qualifications and experience Monthly Type: 11-month Tenure Track: Tenure Full time/Part time: Full-time Temporary/Permanent: Permanent Other Conditions: Appointment to begin on or after July 1, 2027, pending position clearance and availability of funding. The College is seeking qualified individuals with expertise in ambulatory care. This position will be located on the island of Oahu. Note: To ensure a consistent digital layout across third-party software platforms, Hawaiian language (kahako and okina) and other such diacritical marks have been omitted from this online posting. The University recognizes and respects the proper use of these markings in all official communications. Position Overview: The University of Hawaii at Hilo (UH Hilo) is a public university on the Island of Hawaii, recognized for its integration of cultural immersion and place-based research that leverages the island's extraordinary geological, biological, and cultural diversity. A comprehensive, regional campus within the ten-campus University of Hawaii System, UH Hilo offers undergraduate, graduate, and professional degrees that span six colleges: Arts & Sciences; Natural & Health Sciences; Business Administration; Agriculture, Forestry & Natural Resources; Hawaiian Language; and Pharmacy. The colleges are accountable to the Office of the Vice Chancellor for Academic Affairs at UH Hilo, which provides leadership and administrative oversight for the instructional, research, and academic support units of the campus. The Daniel K. Inouye College of Pharmacy (DKICP) is the only pharmacy school in Hawaii and the Pacific Basin. The college is committed to offering quality heath professional education to meet the needs of the state; driving innovations that advance health, science and technology; and providing outreach and services that empower health and well-being in communities. This is a full-time, 11-month, tenure-track Clinical Assistant Professor position that reports to the Chair of the Department of Pharmacy Practice. The ideal candidate will contribute to teaching, research and service, with expertise in ambulatory care. This position is based on Oahu, Hawai?i. Duties and Responsibilities : Contribute to the PharmD curriculum in both the in-person and extended, online PharmD curriculum. Precept students on introductory and/or advanced pharmacy practice experiences. Serve as a faculty mentor and/or advisor for pharmacy students. Establish and maintain a practice site in an ambulatory care clinic. Build and maintain relationships with physicians, administration and staff. Improve system functions. Engage in College, University, and community professional service including service on College of Pharmacy committees. Complete specified activities assigned by the Chair of Pharmacy Practice to support the College of Pharmacy’s mission and accreditation. Participate in scholarly activity that advances pharmacy practice. The expected distribution of effort will be 75% teaching/coordination/practice, 25% service/scholarship. These efforts will be modified as needed based on the needs of the College and the PharmD program and at the discretion of the Dean and Department Chair. Minimum Qualifications: Eligible for Pharmacist licensure in the State of Hawaii at the time of hire PharmD degree from an accredited college or university Successful completion of a PGY1 residency program or equivalent professional experience Willingness to collaborate with other faculty on team taught courses Desirable Qualifications: Five (5) years pharmacy practice experience Pharmacy practice experience of 1-3 years with experience teaching pharmacy students Experience with curricular development, assessment and evaluation in a PharmD program Knowledge of the accreditation standards of the Accreditation Council for Pharmaceutical Education (ACPE) To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach each of the required document. A complete application must include the following as separate documents: Cover letter highlighting the ability to perform the stated duties, how the applicant meets all minimum qualifications and any desirable qualifications, and which summarizes how relevant teaching, patient care, scholarship and service experience would complement the current expertise within the department; Current curriculum vitae; One-to-two page statement of teaching philosophy, including if available, evidence of high-quality teaching in the classroom, laboratory and practice environment; One-to-two page statement of patient care philosophy, including relevance to diverse populations and rural areas prevalent in Hawai?i; Academic transcripts showing degrees and course work appropriate to the position (copies are acceptable; however, official transcripts are required prior to employment); Contact information (names, affiliations, addresses, e-mail addresses, and telephone numbers) of three current professional references. Please do not include any self-identifying photos. Please redact references to Social Security number and birth date on transcript copies. Copy of diplomas will NOT be accepted. Documents issued from an institution outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. Expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. All requested documents/information become the property of the University. All minimum qualifications must be met by the closing date. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Failure to submit all required documents and reference information shall deem an application to be incomplete. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account. Inquiries: Dr. Chad Kawakami, chad.kawakami@hawaii.edu EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Equal Opportunity Employer / Veterans / Disabled Employer University of Hawaii Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply"
    }
  },
